      en: "The National Museum of Cambodia in Phnom Penh is the country's leading historical and archaeological museum, housing one of the world's largest collections of Khmer art. The building itself is a masterpiece of traditional architecture, designed by French architect George Groslier and constructed between 1917 and 1920. It is characterized by its deep red facade, ornate gables, and a peaceful inner courtyard featuring lotus ponds that offer a serene escape from the city. The collection includes more than 14,000 items, spanning from prehistoric times through the glorious Angkorian era to later centuries. Visitors can admire monumental sculptures, bronze works, and ceramics that provide deep insights into the spiritual and artistic development of the Khmer civilization. Particularly striking are the colossal statues of Vishnu and the intricately carved reliefs from the temples of Angkor. As the cultural heart of the capital, the museum plays a vital role in preserving Cambodian heritage and identity following the traumatic decades of conflict in the 20th century.",
