      en: "The Cambodia-Vietnam Friendship Monument is a monumental structure located in the center of Phnom Penh, within the sprawling Botum Park near the Royal Palace. It was erected in the late 1970s following the overthrow of the Khmer Rouge regime by Vietnamese forces in 1979. Designed in the style of socialist realism, the monument depicts two soldiers—one Cambodian and one Vietnamese—jointly protecting a woman holding a child, symbolizing liberation and a fresh start for the nation. The concrete structure, standing approximately 11 meters high, rests on a massive pedestal and is surrounded by well-maintained gardens that are a popular meeting spot for locals in the evening. Despite its political sensitivities, as some view it as a symbol of Vietnamese occupation, it remains a prominent landmark in the city's historical landscape. The site is often used for official commemorative events and is impressively illuminated at night. Visitors can examine the post-war architectural aesthetics and enjoy the relaxed atmosphere of one of the capital's greenest areas.",
